# Fault.Bounds drop-coherence (error-model.md §11 §5): a faultable index read
# of a Drop-bearing element (`Vector[String]`) where the OUT-OF-BOUNDS path is
# taken AND the vector is SUBSEQUENTLY MUTATED.
#
# Regression guard for the Increment-2 resource-coherence crash (Core #8): the
# faultable dst was tagged a CollectionRef into `names`, so the later
# `names.push(...)` ran `cow_before_mutation` and cloned the dst — but on the
# out-of-bounds path the dst is NULL (safe_get returned NULL and control took
# the handler), so the clone NULL-dereffed in `gorget_string_clone_to_owned`,
# crashing identically on C and LLVM. The fault-catch already materializes the
# caught result to an OWNED value, so nothing borrows `names` past the catch —
# the CollectionRef tag is dropped for the faultable read, and the push is safe.
void main():
    Vector[String] names = ["alice", "bob"]
    # OUT-OF-BOUNDS → takes the handler, yields the owned fallback. Then mutate
    # the vector (reallocating its buffer) and USE the caught value: the value
    # must be independent of `names`, and the push must not touch the dead read.
    String b = (names[99]) catch Fault.Bounds: "missing"
    names.push("eve")
    print(b)
    # IN-BOUNDS → yields the element itself (owned clone at the catch boundary).
    # Mutate again and use both the caught value and a fresh read.
    String a = (names[0]) catch Fault.Bounds: "missing"
    names.push("dave")
    print(a)
    print(names[3])
